---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-and-humidity`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin D and Humidity',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  lastReviewed: '2026-05-01',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamin D and Humidity', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin D and Humidity: Does Sweaty Skin Change UVB Absorption?"
  description="Whether humidity affects UVB reaching the ground, sweat interference with vitamin D synthesis, and why tropical humid climates aren't a sun-exposure penalty."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-and-humidity", label: "Humidity" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin D and Humidity</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Humidity's effect on UVB is small. Atmospheric water vapour absorbs some UV
      but the effect is dwarfed by cloud cover, latitude, and time of day. The
      practical relevance for vitamin D is minimal — humid tropical climates
      generally still deliver plentiful UVB.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Water vapour and UVB</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Water vapour absorbs UV weakly across the 290–315 nm band relevant to vitamin D
      synthesis. Even in very humid conditions (dew point &gt; 24 °C), the reduction
      in ground-level UVB is at most 3–8% versus a dry-air reference — negligible
      compared with a 30–50% reduction from moderate cloud cover.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Sweat and skin synthesis</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      A common myth: showering right after sun exposure "washes off" freshly made
      vitamin D. Not true. Cholecalciferol is produced inside the epidermis (not on
      the skin surface) from 7-dehydrocholesterol, and it gradually diffuses into
      the bloodstream over hours to a day. Sweat, water, and soap do not remove it.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The behavioural angle</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      What humidity does affect is behaviour — hot humid climates drive people
      indoors during midday hours. Bangkok, Miami, Singapore all have high UVB
      years-round but urban deficiency rates that rival northern climates because
      residents avoid outdoor midday time. See our
      <a href="/vitamin-d-and-hot-climates" class="text-amber-700 underline">hot climates page</a>.
    </p>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> Don't worry about humidity as a vitamin
      D variable. Worry about behaviour (indoor time), clothing, and latitude.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-and-humidity" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
